{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789223832,"nonce":"muV10nb4mhOXeUXrgtGcD9xuuOM=","title":"A folded comparison is recorded at its operand type, not Bool","message":"`const_fold::try_fold_inst` returns the instruction's `ty` as the type\nof the folded value. For a comparison that is the operand type (the\n`Binary` instruction carries it so the backend knows signedness), so\n`1 \u003c 2` folds to `HirConstant::Bool(true)` recorded as `i64`. Anything\nthat keys off the value's type afterwards reads a boolean as an\ninteger: boxing it into a dynamic slot tags it Int, and it prints as\n`1`. With `ZYNTAX_DISABLE_INTERP_OPTS=1` the same program prints\n`true`, which is how the two disagree.\n\nReproducer (Python frontend): `print(1 \u003c 2)` prints `1` under the\ndefault pipeline and `true` with folding off. The ZynML equivalent\n`println(1 \u003c 2)` shows the same split.\n\nThe result value already has the right type when the instruction was\nbuilt; the fold must not overwrite it with the operand's. Same shape\napplies to `Unary` and `Select` only if their `ty` can differ from the\nresult's, which for `Not` on a Bool it does not.","files":null}]}